steps to improve database efficiency to get into production level

1) Database Layer (PostgreSQL)
Indexes

Primary keys (already there).

Foreign keys (items.adviceId, qualityDiffs.adviceId).

Business filters (partyName, adviceNo, date, paymentMode).

Composite indexes if queries combine filters (e.g., partyName + date).

Pagination queries

Always use LIMIT + OFFSET or better: keyset pagination (WHERE id > last_seen_id LIMIT 20).

Example:

sql
SELECT * FROM advices 
WHERE partyName ILIKE $1 
ORDER BY date DESC 
LIMIT 20 OFFSET 0;
Search optimization

Use ILIKE for case‑insensitive search.

For advanced search, add a GIN index on text fields (e.g., partyName) for fast full‑text search.

2) 🎨 Frontend Layer (React + Tailwind)
Virtualized tables

Use react-window or react-virtualized so only visible rows render.

This prevents the browser from choking on thousands of DOM nodes.

Debounced search

Add a 300ms debounce on filter inputs so you don’t hit the DB on every keystroke.

Pagination UI

Keep “Rows per page” selector. Default to 20–50 rows.

Show page numbers or infinite scroll with “Load more.”

Loading states

Show spinners or skeleton loaders while queries run.

Users feel the app is responsive even if queries take 200–300ms.

3) ⚙️ Backend Layer (Electron + Node)
IPC handlers

Ensure handlers (getAdvices, deleteAdvice) always use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection.

Example:

js
const res = await pool.query(
  'SELECT * FROM advices WHERE partyName ILIKE $1 LIMIT $2 OFFSET $3',
  [`%${partyName}%`, limit, offset]
);
Error handling

Return meaningful error messages to frontend (e.g., “No records found” vs. generic error).

Caching (optional)

For frequently accessed reports, consider caching results in Redis.

4) 📦 CI/CD + Testing
Unit tests for CRUD (Jest).

UI tests for dashboard actions (Playwright).

GitHub Actions pipeline: run tests → build Electron app → upload .exe.

Release management: publish .exe to GitHub Releases for users.

🚀 User Experience Outcome
Searching 100k records feels instant because queries hit indexes.

Dashboard loads only 20–50 rows at a time, no sluggish scrolling.

Filters respond smoothly with debounce.

Export to Excel works on filtered data, not the entire dataset at once.

✅ If you follow these steps, your dashboard will feel production‑grade, responsive, and scalable even with lakhs of records.

